ull,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>A digital computer has a memory unit with 32 bits per word. The instruction set consists of 110 different operations. All instructions have an operation code part (opcode) and two address fields: one for a memory address and one for a register address. This particular system includes eight general-purpose, user-addressable registers. Registers may be loaded directly from memory, and memory may be updated directly from the registers. Direct memory-to-memory data movement operations are not supported. Each instruction is stored in one word of memory. If X, Y, Z represent the number of bits that are needed for the opcode, for the register field, and bits that are left for the memory address part of the instruction, respectively, then XY + YZ is ________.</p>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"99ad4557-dfb4-4683-a114-7d32502c1580","journalQuestionId":"6029a694-d2d5-4388-ba33-ef83df1c3355","reattemptId":"02932575-c226-44a6-bafd-6387011d4b84","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:22:34.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:22:42.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":8000,"number":"3","selectedAnswer":"C","decision":"MARK","markCorrect":true,"timeSpentSec":8,"outcome":"R","questionText":"The unsigned integer 3,505,468,161 can be written in 32-bit binary as 11010000 11110001 00110011 00000001. Putting it into four bytes of memory beginning at address 98370 in little-endian fashion would give which picture?\nA. 98370 | 98371 | 98372 | 98373\n11010000 | 11110001 | 00110011 | 00000001\nB. 98370 | 98371 | 98072 | 98373\n00000001 | 11110001 | 00110011 | 11010000\nC. 98370 | 98371 | 98372 | 98373\n00000001 | 00110011 | 11110001 | 11010000\nD. 98370 | 98371 | 98372 | 98373\n00110011 | 00000001 | 11010000 | 11110001","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:3","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"3","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"machine-instruction"},"topic":"Machine Instruction","topicSlug":"machine-instruction","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","machine-instructions","data-representation","little-endian","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":1,"type":"MCQ","answer":"C","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>The unsigned integer 3,505,468,161 can be written in 32-bit binary as <code>11010000 11110001 00110011 00000001</code>. Putting it into four bytes of memory beginning at address 98370 in little-endian fashion would give which picture?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li><table><tr><th>98370</th><th>98371</th><th>98372</th><th>98373</th></tr><tr><td>11010000</td><td>11110001</td><td>00110011</td><td>00000001</td></tr></table></li><li><table><tr><th>98370</th><th>98371</th><th>98072</th><th>98373</th></tr><tr><td>00000001</td><td>11110001</td><td>00110011</td><td>11010000</td></tr></table></li><li><table><tr><th>98370</th><th>98371</th><th>98372</th><th>98373</th></tr><tr><td>00000001</td><td>00110011</td><td>11110001</td><td>11010000</td></tr></table></li><li><table><tr><th>98370</th><th>98371</th><th>98372</th><th>98373</th></tr><tr><td>00110011</td><td>00000001</td><td>11010000</td><td>11110001</td></tr></table></li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"2c255e6d-6df6-4e96-b2e9-262c3c4759eb","journalQuestionId":"b74d2fee-8053-43d6-ad05-41e60cd747bc","reattemptId":"233edeed-2e47-46e9-8ddf-969e45a20923","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:22:47.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:23:44.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":57000,"number":"4","selectedAnswer":["A","B"],"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":true,"timeSpentSec":57,"outcome":"R","questionText":"When performing hardware integer arithmetic, we say that overflow has occurred when the mathematically correct result of a computation cannot be represented in the number of bits available for the type being used. Obviously, it is important to be able to detect when an overflow error has occurred.\nFor the following options, the bit-sequences are 16-bit 2's complement representations of (signed) integer values. For which of the following does an overflow occur when the given two integers are added?\nA. 0111 1001 1011 1011 + 0011 1011 1110 1110\nB. 1111 0111 0110 1001 + 1000 0001 0110 0100\nC. 0001 1100 0110 1111 + 1111 0111 1110 1101\nD. 1100 1011 1010 1101 + 0111 1111 0010 1111","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:4","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"4","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit"},"topic":"ALU Data Path and Control Unit","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","computer-arithmetic","twos-complement","signed-overflow","multiple-selects","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":1,"type":"MSQ","answer":["A","B"],"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>When performing hardware integer arithmetic, we say that overflow has occurred when the mathematically correct result of a computation cannot be represented in the number of bits available for the type being used. Obviously, it is important to be able to detect when an overflow error has occurred.</p><p>For the following options, the bit-sequences are 16-bit 2's complement representations of (signed) integer values. For which of the following does an overflow occur when the given two integers are added?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li><code>0111 1001 1011 1011 + 0011 1011 1110 1110</code></li><li><code>1111 0111 0110 1001 + 1000 0001 0110 0100</code></li><li><code>0001 1100 0110 1111 + 1111 0111 1110 1101</code></li><li><code>1100 1011 1010 1101 + 0111 1111 0010 1111</code></li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"bf3b9a4d-d7ba-4c01-8cd6-2270d6f0269d","journalQuestionId":"c6c41755-f197-4056-a93b-39fd3053beca","reattemptId":"908eb698-fd22-4ba2-8b4b-983a7cbbe8bb","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:23:49.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:27:38.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":229000,"number":"5","selectedAnswer":["B"],"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":false,"timeSpentSec":229,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"Consider the control unit that adopts the single address field branch control logic. Assume that the control word is 24 bits wide. The control portion of the micro-instruction format is divided into two fields. A micro-operation field of 13 bits specifies the micro-operations to be performed. An address selection field specifies 8 conditions that will cause a micro-instruction branch.\nWhich of the following is/are true?\nA. 8 bits are in the address selection field.\nB. 8 bits are in the address field.\nC. The size of the control memory is 768 Bytes.\nD. The size of the control memory is 4096 bits.","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:5","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"5","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit"},"topic":"ALU Data Path and Control Unit","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","control-unit","microprogrammed-control","control-memory","multiple-selects","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":1,"type":"MSQ","answer":["B","C"],"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Consider the control unit that adopts the single address field branch control logic. Assume that the control word is 24 bits wide. The control portion of the micro-instruction format is divided into two fields. A micro-operation field of 13 bits specifies the micro-operations to be performed. An address selection field specifies 8 conditions that will cause a micro-instruction branch.</p><p>Which of the following is/are true?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>8 bits are in the address selection field.</li><li>8 bits are in the address field.</li><li>The size of the control memory is 768 Bytes.</li><li>The size of the control memory is 4096 bits.</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"52fab056-63ca-4677-b1c7-a7d68f17a89c","journalQuestionId":"1e9fe58d-35d4-44f8-a935-8031199863db","reattemptId":"49f60218-6049-46ce-9bff-4a591dae29c0","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:27:43.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:32:57.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":314000,"number":"6","selectedAnswer":"B","decision":"MARK","markCorrect":true,"timeSpentSec":314,"outcome":"RBS","questionText":"A CPU has an arithmetic unit that adds bytes and then sets its V, C, and Z flag bits as follows. The V-bit is set if arithmetic overflow occurs (in two's complement arithmetic). The C-bit is set if a carry-out is generated from the most significant bit during an operation. The Z-bit is set if the result is zero. What are the values of the V, C, and Z flag bits (in that order) after the 8-bit bytes 1100 1100 and 1000 1111 are added?\nA. 000\nB. 110\nC. 111\nD. 001","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:6","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"6","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit"},"topic":"ALU Data Path and Control Unit","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","computer-arithmetic","condition-flags","overflow-and-carry","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","answer":"B","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>A CPU has an arithmetic unit that adds bytes and then sets its V, C, and Z flag bits as follows. The V-bit is set if arithmetic overflow occurs (in two's complement arithmetic). The C-bit is set if a carry-out is generated from the most significant bit during an operation. The Z-bit is set if the result is zero. What are the values of the V, C, and Z flag bits (in that order) after the 8-bit bytes <code>1100 1100</code> and <code>1000 1111</code> are added?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>000</li><li>110</li><li>111</li><li>001</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"e5e4b1cf-9138-49dc-8181-531a35f74dd8","journalQuestionId":"6b71192d-2ccc-4c12-8bfc-605c27f76b2d","reattemptId":"b6842007-8097-4e57-a898-c45f4a0545b4","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:33:02.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:34:03.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":61000,"number":"7","selectedAnswer":"C","decision":"MARK","markCorrect":true,"timeSpentSec":61,"outcome":"R","questionText":"Consider the instruction Load R5, X(R7), which uses the Index addressing mode to load a word of data from memory location X + [R7] into register R5.\nExecution of this instruction involves the following actions:\n(I1) Fetch the instruction from the memory.\n(I2) Decode the instruction to determine the operation to be performed.\n(I3) Add the immediate value X to the contents of R7.\n(I4) Read register R7.\n(I5) Use the sum X + [R7] as the effective address of the source operand, and read the contents of that location in the memory.\n(I6) Increment the program counter.\n(I7) Load the data received from the memory into the destination register, R5.\nWhich of the following is the most preferred order of execution of the above instruction?\nA. I1 → I2 → I3 → I4 → I5 → I6 → I7\nB. I1 → I2 → I6 → I3 → I4 → I5 → I7\nC. I1 → I6 → I2 → I4 → I3 → I5 → I7\nD. I1 → I6 → I2 → I5 → I4 → I3 → I7","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:7","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"7","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"addressing-modes"},"topic":"Addressing Modes","topicSlug":"addressing-modes","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","addressing-modes","indexed-addressing","instruction-cycle","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","answer":"C","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Consider the instruction <strong>Load R5, X(R7)</strong>, which uses the Index addressing mode to load a word of data from memory location X + [R7] into register R5.</p><p>Execution of this instruction involves the following actions:</p><ul><li>(I1) Fetch the instruction from the memory.</li><li>(I2) Decode the instruction to determine the operation to be performed.</li><li>(I3) Add the immediate value X to the contents of R7.</li><li>(I4) Read register R7.</li><li>(I5) Use the sum X + [R7] as the effective address of the source operand, and read the contents of that location in the memory.</li><li>(I6) Increment the program counter.</li><li>(I7) Load the data received from the memory into the destination register, R5.</li></ul><p>Which of the following is the most preferred order of execution of the above instruction?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>I1 → I2 → I3 → I4 → I5 → I6 → I7</li><li>I1 → I2 → I6 → I3 → I4 → I5 → I7</li><li>I1 → I6 → I2 → I4 → I3 → I5 → I7</li><li>I1 → I6 → I2 → I5 → I4 → I3 → I7</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"782068f3-6907-48ec-9c76-92b9aafc3f61","journalQuestionId":"1c57eb86-f047-4b16-999b-03e68bd1feae","reattemptId":"079ea94b-93b8-484b-a166-bf1be512ddba","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:34:08.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:35:15.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":67000,"number":"8","selectedAnswer":"A","decision":"MARK","markCorrect":true,"timeSpentSec":67,"outcome":"R","questionText":"The following table specifies micro-operations and active control signals:\nCycle | Time | Micro-operation | Active control signals\nFetch | $t_1$ | MAR ← (PC) | $C_2$\n$t_2$ | MBR ← Memory\nPC ← (PC) + 1 | $C_5, C_R$\n$t_3$ | IR ← (MBR) | $C_4$\nIndirect | $t_1$ | MAR ← (IR(Address)) | $C_8$\n$t_2$ | MBR ← Memory | $C_5, C_R$\n$t_3$ | IR(Address) ← (MBR(Address)) | $C_4$\nInterrupt | $t_1$ | MBR ← (PC) | $C_1$\n$t_2$ | MAR ← Save-address\nPC ← Routine-address |\n$t_3$ | Memory ← (MBR) | $C_{12}, C_W$\n$C_R$ is the read control signal to the system bus, and $C_W$ is the write control signal to the system bus.\nLet us define control signals P and Q with the following interpretation:\nPQ = 00: Fetch cycle\nPQ = 01: Indirect cycle\nPQ = 10: Execute cycle\nPQ = 11: Interrupt cycle\nWhich of the following Boolean expressions defines $C_4$?\nA. $P't_3$\nB. $Pt'_3 + Q't_3$\nC. $P'Q't_3 + PQ't_3$\nD. $P'Q't_3 + PQt_3$","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:8","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"8","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit"},"topic":"ALU Data Path and Control Unit","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","control-unit","instruction-cycle","control-signals","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","answer":"A","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>The following table specifies micro-operations and active control signals:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Cycle</th><th>Time</th><th>Micro-operation</th><th>Active control signals</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Fetch</td><td>$t_1$</td><td>MAR ← (PC)</td><td>$C_2$</td></tr><tr><td>$t_2$</td><td>MBR ← Memory<br>PC ← (PC) + 1</td><td>$C_5, C_R$</td></tr><tr><td>$t_3$</td><td>IR ← (MBR)</td><td>$C_4$</td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Indirect</td><td>$t_1$</td><td>MAR ← (IR(Address))</td><td>$C_8$</td></tr><tr><td>$t_2$</td><td>MBR ← Memory</td><td>$C_5, C_R$</td></tr><tr><td>$t_3$</td><td>IR(Address) ← (MBR(Address))</td><td>$C_4$</td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Interrupt</td><td>$t_1$</td><td>MBR ← (PC)</td><td>$C_1$</td></tr><tr><td>$t_2$</td><td>MAR ← Save-address<br>PC ← Routine-address</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>$t_3$</td><td>Memory ← (MBR)</td><td>$C_{12}, C_W$</td></tr></tbody></table><p>$C_R$ is the read control signal to the system bus, and $C_W$ is the write control signal to the system bus.</p><p>Let us define control signals P and Q with the following interpretation:</p><ul><li>PQ = 00: Fetch cycle</li><li>PQ = 01: Indirect cycle</li><li>PQ = 10: Execute cycle</li><li>PQ = 11: Interrupt cycle</li></ul><p>Which of the following Boolean expressions defines $C_4$?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$P't_3$</li><li>$Pt'_3 + Q't_3$</li><li>$P'Q't_3 + PQ't_3$</li><li>$P'Q't_3 + PQt_3$</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"0bdea37e-1237-45bd-8295-e4fd0afa2423","journalQuestionId":"afe7da0b-3f95-4a56-8d8e-1420dee3575c","reattemptId":"749d4cd7-e5da-47cb-aa3f-bae63973b05f","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:35:20.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:38:05.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":165000,"number":"9","selectedAnswer":"B","decision":"MARK","markCorrect":true,"timeSpentSec":165,"outcome":"R","questionText":"The 8-bit registers M, N, O and P initially have the following values:\n$M=10001111; N=01100010; O=01001001; P=01110010$\nThe following assembly code is executed:\nM ← M ⊕ N\nM ← CSL M\nN ← M + N\nO ← O ∧ N\nO ← CSR O\nP ← P + 1\nP ← P + O\nDetermine the 8-bit values in each register after the execution of the above sequence of micro-operations. CSL: Circular shift left; CSR: Circular shift right; ∧: logical AND; +: Arithmetic addition; ⊕: logical Ex-or.\nA. M = 11101101; N = 00111101; O = 10000100; P = 11110111\nB. M = 11011011; N = 00111101; O = 10000100; P = 11110111\nC. M = 11011011; N = 10111101; O = 10000100; P = 11110111\nD. M = 11011011; N = 00111101; O = 00001001; P = 01110011","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:9","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"9","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit"},"topic":"ALU Data Path and Control Unit","topicSlug":"alu-data-path-and-control-unit","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","register-transfer-language","micro-operations","circular-shifts","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","answer":"B","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>The 8-bit registers M, N, O and P initially have the following values:</p><p>$M=10001111; N=01100010; O=01001001; P=01110010$</p><p>The following assembly code is executed:</p><ul><li>M ← M ⊕ N</li><li>M ← CSL M</li><li>N ← M + N</li><li>O ← O ∧ N</li><li>O ← CSR O</li><li>P ← P + 1</li><li>P ← P + O</li></ul><p>Determine the 8-bit values in each register after the execution of the above sequence of micro-operations. CSL: Circular shift left; CSR: Circular shift right; ∧: logical AND; +: Arithmetic addition; ⊕: logical Ex-or.</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>M = 11101101; N = 00111101; O = 10000100; P = 11110111</li><li>M = 11011011; N = 00111101; O = 10000100; P = 11110111</li><li>M = 11011011; N = 10111101; O = 10000100; P = 11110111</li><li>M = 11011011; N = 00111101; O = 00001001; P = 01110011</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"79bf54a7-7648-46b3-b033-f82c6e8b13ea","journalQuestionId":"5f73894d-8202-4bef-8346-a92c8581471b","reattemptId":"8401e0e0-1697-41b6-8fbd-c912146e9d8d","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:38:10.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:39:52.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":102000,"number":"10","selectedAnswer":"B","decision":"MARK","markCorrect":false,"timeSpentSec":102,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"A particular parallel program computation requires 100 seconds when executed on a single processor. If 40 percent of this computation is “inherently sequential” (i.e., will not benefit from additional processors), then the theoretically best possible elapsed times for this program running with 2 and 4 processors, respectively, are\nA. 20 seconds and 10 seconds\nB. 50 seconds and 25 seconds\nC. 70 seconds and 55 seconds\nD. 80 seconds and 70 seconds","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:10","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"10","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"pipeline-processor"},"topic":"Pipeline Processor","topicSlug":"pipeline-processor","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","processor-performance","amdahls-law","parallel-speedup","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","answer":"C","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>A particular parallel program computation requires 100 seconds when executed on a single processor. If 40 percent of this computation is “inherently sequential” (i.e., will not benefit from additional processors), then the theoretically best possible elapsed times for this program running with 2 and 4 processors, respectively, are</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>20 seconds and 10 seconds</li><li>50 seconds and 25 seconds</li><li>70 seconds and 55 seconds</li><li>80 seconds and 70 seconds</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"e6700856-f1c9-46d2-afa7-881c24471068","journalQuestionId":"3188c826-8566-4336-b13b-7dc222d580b3","reattemptId":"17e71de5-5145-46ad-acd7-ce459ac65b5d","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:39:57.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:42:04.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":127000,"number":"11","selectedAnswer":96,"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":true,"timeSpentSec":127,"outcome":"R","questionText":"A 64-bit processor has 64 registers and uses a 20-bit instruction format. It has two types of instructions: M-type and R-type. Each M-type instruction contains an opcode and a memory address. Each R-type instruction contains an opcode and two register names. Main memory is 64K Bytes, and it is byte addressable. If there are 10 distinct M-type opcodes, then the maximum number of distinct R-type opcodes is ________.","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:11","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"11","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"machine-instruction"},"topic":"Machine Instruction","topicSlug":"machine-instruction","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","machine-instructions","instruction-format","opcode-encoding","numerical-answers","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"NAT","answer":96,"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>A 64-bit processor has 64 registers and uses a 20-bit instruction format. It has two types of instructions: M-type and R-type. Each M-type instruction contains an opcode and a memory address. Each R-type instruction contains an opcode and two register names. Main memory is 64K Bytes, and it is byte addressable. If there are 10 distinct M-type opcodes, then the maximum number of distinct R-type opcodes is ________.</p>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"533be28f-c3db-4139-892c-940132ca81e9","journalQuestionId":"b564aef8-ffd8-4cbe-b78d-d7d608883b6b","reattemptId":"aabdc30a-9751-4254-bc40-7d13c0151654","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:42:09.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:43:34.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":85000,"number":"12","selectedAnswer":null,"decision":"SKIP","markCorrect":null,"timeSpentSec":85,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"Amdahl's Law pertains to the speedup achieved when running a program on parallel processors versus using a single serial processor. In this context, the speedup is the ratio of original running time to improved running time. According to Amdahl's Law, approximately how much speedup could we expect for an unlimited number of processors if 10 percent of a program is sequential (i.e., will not benefit from additional processors) and the remaining part is ideally parallel?\nA. 10X\nB. 20X\nC. 40X\nD. Infinite","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:12","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"12","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"pipeline-processor"},"topic":"Pipeline Processor","topicSlug":"pipeline-processor","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","processor-performance","amdahls-law","parallel-speedup","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","answer":"A","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Amdahl's Law pertains to the speedup achieved when running a program on parallel processors versus using a single serial processor. In this context, the speedup is the ratio of original running time to improved running time. According to Amdahl's Law, approximately how much speedup could we expect for an unlimited number of processors if 10 percent of a program is sequential (i.e., will not benefit from additional processors) and the remaining part is ideally parallel?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>10X</li><li>20X</li><li>40X</li><li>Infinite</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"c05eded3-f37b-40fe-98d2-325c2ae0f9ca","journalQuestionId":"c542a136-6733-44a1-8e27-30aff9f803b7","reattemptId":"3d84ae0c-b6c3-4733-ab28-08fced81f816","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:43:39.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:43:55.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":16000,"number":"13","selectedAnswer":null,"decision":"SKIP","markCorrect":null,"timeSpentSec":16,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"Following is a definition of a widget and a declaration of an array A that contains 10 widgets. The sizes of a byte, short, int, and long are 1, 2, 4, and 8 bytes, respectively. Alignment is restricted so that an n-byte field must be located at an address divisible by n. The fields in a struct are not rearranged; padding is used to ensure alignment. All widgets in A must have the same size.\nstruct widget\nshort s\nbyte b\nlong l\nint i\nend widget\nwidget A[10]\nAssuming that A is located at a memory address divisible by 8, what is the total size of A, in bytes?\nA. 150\nB. 160\nC. 200\nD. 240","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:13","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"13","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"machine-instruction"},"topic":"Machine Instruction","topicSlug":"machine-instruction","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","data-layout","structure-padding","memory-alignment","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","answer":"D","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Following is a definition of a <strong>widget</strong> and a declaration of an array A that contains 10 widgets. The sizes of a byte, short, int, and long are 1, 2, 4, and 8 bytes, respectively. Alignment is restricted so that an n-byte field must be located at an address divisible by n. The fields in a struct are not rearranged; padding is used to ensure alignment. All widgets in A must have the same size.</p><pre>struct widget\n    short s\n    byte b\n    long l\n    int i\nend widget\n\nwidget A[10]</pre><p>Assuming that A is located at a memory address divisible by 8, what is the total size of A, in bytes?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>150</li><li>160</li><li>200</li><li>240</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"c504758b-2785-4858-a10c-c72fa4a7ddcf","journalQuestionId":"ac22c137-82f9-4fa8-a2be-f408fa783f80","reattemptId":"38048e2b-7cc5-4aaf-a192-6c53920bc8c3","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:44:00.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:46:00.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":120000,"number":"14","selectedAnswer":null,"decision":"SKIP","markCorrect":null,"timeSpentSec":120,"outcome":"W-C","questionText":"Consider two different design enhancements of ALU as follows:\n1. A part of a bigger task is improved twenty times than it was before. The other part of the same task constitutes 60% of the overall task time, and it remains unchanged.\n2. The designer can make changes to improve 20% of the task 100% faster, 35% of the task 4 times faster, and 10% of the task 100 times faster, but it causes the remaining part of the task to perform as bad as 50% slower than before.\nWhich of the following values is the best approximate difference between the two speedups achieved in those two improvements?\nA. 0.3567\nB. 0.2667\nC. 0.4875\nD. 0.4325","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:14","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"14","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"pipeline-processor"},"topic":"Pipeline Processor","topicSlug":"pipeline-processor","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","processor-performance","amdahls-law","speedup","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"MCQ","answer":"C","tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Consider two different design enhancements of ALU as follows:</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:lower-roman\"><li>A part of a bigger task is improved twenty times than it was before. The other part of the same task constitutes 60% of the overall task time, and it remains unchanged.</li><li>The designer can make changes to improve 20% of the task 100% faster, 35% of the task 4 times faster, and 10% of the task 100 times faster, but it causes the remaining part of the task to perform as bad as 50% slower than before.</li></ol><p>Which of the following values is the best approximate difference between the two speedups achieved in those two improvements?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>0.3567</li><li>0.2667</li><li>0.4875</li><li>0.4325</li></ol>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}},{"attemptId":"328186e7-d3e0-484c-9418-94e20345c875","journalQuestionId":"6e53f251-ad58-4010-ae8d-b144c5ba1113","reattemptId":"cb6fb282-259f-4c57-99e4-4314f37d407c","questionStartedAt":"2026-08-13T06:46:05.000Z","attemptedAt":"2026-08-13T06:48:44.000Z","scheduledDate":"2026-08-16","timeSpentMs":159000,"number":"15","selectedAnswer":82032,"decision":"MARK","markCorrect":true,"timeSpentSec":159,"outcome":"R","questionText":"Big-Endian (BE) and Little-Endian (LE) change the order in which the bytes of a word are stored in RAM. We typically show the contents of a word, especially if it is an integer, as four hexadecimal pairs of characters. For example, the hex string 0xabcdef12 contains four bytes: ab is the first, which has the binary value 1010 and 1011, so the first 8 bits are 10101011. BE/LE specifies whether this byte goes into RAM as the first of the four bytes in the word or as the last. Suppose Byte 0 in RAM contains the value 0x00. Subsequent bytes contain 0x01, 0x40, and 0x70. On a Big-Endian system with a 32-bit word, what is the decimal value of the word?","question":{"id":"goclasses:coa-topic-test-2:15","year":2026,"set":null,"number":"15","paperLabel":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","subject":"Computer Organization","subjectSlug":"coa","classificationHint":{"subjectSlug":"coa","topicSlug":"machine-instruction"},"topic":"Machine Instruction","topicSlug":"machine-instruction","subtopics":["co-and-architecture","machine-instructions","data-representation","big-endian","numerical-answers","goclasses-coa-topic-test-2"],"marks":2,"type":"NAT","answer":82032,"tolerance":null,"answerStatus":"available","html":"<p>Big-Endian (BE) and Little-Endian (LE) change the order in which the bytes of a word are stored in RAM. We typically show the contents of a word, especially if it is an integer, as four hexadecimal pairs of characters. For example, the hex string <code>0xabcdef12</code> contains four bytes: <code>ab</code> is the first, which has the binary value 1010 and 1011, so the first 8 bits are 10101011. BE/LE specifies whether this byte goes into RAM as the first of the four bytes in the word or as the last. Suppose Byte 0 in RAM contains the value <code>0x00</code>. Subsequent bytes contain <code>0x01</code>, <code>0x40</code>, and <code>0x70</code>. On a Big-Endian system with a 32-bit word, what is the decimal value of the word?</p>","sourceUrl":"https://www.goclasses.in/","answerSource":{"kind":"learner-provided-screenshot","label":"GO Classes COA Topic Test 2","capturedAt":"2026-08-13"}}}]$payload$::jsonb)
  loop
    insert into public.pyq_attempts (
      id, user_id, pyq_session_id, question_uid, subject, year, attempt_number,
      selected_answer, correct_answer, capture_version, question_snapshot,
      answer_status, screenshot_url, mark_decision, mark_correct,
      question_started_at, time_spent_ms, time_spent_sec, bank_version, attempted_at
    ) values (
      (item->>'attemptId')::uuid,
      target_user_id,
      null,
      item#>>'{question,id}',
      item#>>'{question,subject}',
      (item#>>'{question,year}')::int,
      1,
      case when item->'selectedAnswer' = 'null'::jsonb then null else item->'selectedAnswer' end,
      item#>'{question,answer}',
      2,
      jsonb_build_object(
        'question_uid', item#>>'{question,id}',
        'year', (item#>>'{question,year}')::int,
        'set', item#>'{question,set}',
        'number', item#>>'{question,number}',
        'paper_label', item#>>'{question,paperLabel}',
        'subject', item#>>'{question,subject}',
        'subject_slug', item#>>'{question,subjectSlug}',
        'topic', item#>>'{question,topic}',
        'topic_slug', item#>>'{question,topicSlug}',
        'subtopics', item#>'{question,subtopics}',
        'marks', (item#>>'{question,marks}')::int,
        'type', item#>>'{question,type}',
        'tolerance', item#>'{question,tolerance}',
        'answer_status', item#>>'{question,answerStatus}',
        'answer_source', item#>'{question,answerSource}',
        'html', item#>>'{question,html}',
        'source_url', item#>>'{question,sourceUrl}'
      ),
      item#>>'{question,answerStatus}',
      null,
      (item->>'decision')::mark_decision_t,
      case when item->'markCorrect' = 'null'::jsonb then null else (item->>'markCorrect')::boolean end,
      (item->>'questionStartedAt')::timestamptz,
      (item->>'timeSpentMs')::int,
      (item->>'timeSpentSec')::int,
      'gate-1990-2026-v7-go-classes-coa-topic-tests',
      (item->>'attemptedAt')::timestamptz
    )
    on conflict (id) do nothing;

    insert into public.questions (
      id, user_id, session_id, subject, subtopic, source_year, source_ref,
      question_text, answer_text, image_url, time_spent_sec, target_time_sec,
      outcome, pattern_name, trigger_sentence, root_cause, mark_decision,
      mark_correct, created_at
    ) values (
      (item->>'journalQuestionId')::uuid,
      target_user_id,
      null,
      item#>>'{question,subject}',
      item#>>'{question,topic}',
      (item#>>'{question,year}')::int,
      (item#>>'{question,paperLabel}') || ' · Q' || (item->>'number') || ' · ' || (item#>>'{question,type}'),
      item->>'questionText',
      'Your answer: ' || case
        when item->'selectedAnswer' = 'null'::jsonb then 'Not attempted'
        when jsonb_typeof(item->'selectedAnswer') = 'array'
          then array_to_string(array(select jsonb_array_elements_text(item->'selectedAnswer')), ', ')
        else item->'selectedAnswer' #>> '{}'
      end || E'\nActual answer: ' || case
        when item#>'{question,answer}' = 'null'::jsonb then 'Not attempted'
        when jsonb_typeof(item#>'{question,answer}') = 'array'
          then array_to_string(array(select jsonb_array_elements_text(item#>'{question,answer}')), ', ')
        else item#>'{question,answer}' #>> '{}'
      end,
      null,
      (item->>'timeSpentSec')::int,
      case when (item#>>'{question,marks}')::int = 1 then 90 else 180 end,
      (item->>'outcome')::outcome_t,
      null,
      null,
      null,
      (item->>'decision')::mark_decision_t,
      case when item->'markCorrect' = 'null'::jsonb then null else (item->>'markCorrect')::boolean end,
      (item->>'attemptedAt')::timestamptz
    )
    on conflict (id) do nothing;

    insert into public.reattempts (
      id, user_id, question_id, scheduled_date, stage, history, created_at
    ) values (
      (item->>'reattemptId')::uuid,
      target_user_id,
      (item->>'journalQuestionId')::uuid,
      (item->>'scheduledDate')::date,
      'D3',
      '[]'::jsonb,
      (item->>'attemptedAt')::timestamptz
    )
    on conflict (id) do nothing;
  end loop;
end
